---
/**
 * olx.astro — OLX Brasil case study (route: /work/olx)
 * Primary story: S002 — Ad Platform Migration
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  company="OLX Brasil"
  role="Senior Product Manager"
  period="Feb 2021 — Jun 2024"
  tags={["Marketplace", "Platform Engineering", "APIs", "B2B"]}
  metric="-80% development steps"
  metricNote="Most complex use case: from ~20 steps to 4"
>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">Context</span>
    <h2>A dominant classifieds marketplace with a platform problem</h2>
    <p>
      OLX Brasil is the largest classifieds marketplace in Brazil — cars, real estate,
      jobs, and general goods. At the scale I joined, the company was running multiple
      product verticals simultaneously, each with its own squad and roadmap.
    </p>
    <p>
      I was PM for the Ad Management and Ad Creation squads — the teams responsible for
      the core "ad" entity that everything else in the marketplace was built on top of.
      The ad entity was fragmented across approximately five legacy microservices, each
      owned by a different squad, each with its own review queue and codebase conventions.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">Problem</span>
    <h2>Every new use case required touching infrastructure nobody owned end-to-end</h2>
    <p>
      Adding a new product category, changing a listing field, or launching a new vertical
      feature all required coordinating changes across those five services sequentially.
      Each step took between one and three days — not because the work was complex, but
      because each service had a different owning squad, different code conventions to
      navigate, and a separate review queue to wait in.
    </p>
    <p>
      The most complex use cases required approximately 20 sequential steps to deploy.
      Vertical teams — the squads responsible for autos, real estate, goods — couldn't
      ship independently. Their roadmaps were blocked by infrastructure they didn't own
      and couldn't change.
    </p>
    <p>
      The business consequence was visible: the commercial roadmap (new vertical features,
      new subscription types, B2B automotive tools) was consistently slipping. Sales was
      promising timelines product couldn't deliver.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">Process</span>
    <h2>Solving the management problem before the technical one</h2>
    <p>
      Platform investments are long by nature — months of build with no visible
      user-facing output. In my experience, the biggest failure mode in a platform
      investment isn't technical; it's losing stakeholder buy-in halfway through when
      quarterly reviews come around and there's nothing to show yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      My first move was to create a measurement system before touching any code. I mapped
      existing use case workflows step by step and defined "workflow steps" as the proxy
      metric — each step representing one to three days of real engineering work. This
      gave us two things: a concrete baseline (the most complex use case was 20 steps),
      and a tool for showing measurable progress at each iteration rather than asking
      management for trust until full delivery. Watching a specific use case drop from
      20 steps to 12 to 7 to 4 is concrete. Architecture diagrams are not.
    </p>
    <p>
      In parallel, I ran discovery sessions with every vertical PM to map their forward
      roadmap — ensuring the architecture we designed would support what teams needed
      to build next, not just what existed today.
    </p>
    <p>
      On adoption, I knew a voluntary migration wouldn't work at the pace we needed.
      Once the new platform could support a given use case, I enforced a policy: no
      updates accepted via the legacy path. Exceptions required director-level escalation.
      This made the path of least resistance the new pattern, not a choice.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">Solution</span>
    <h2>Clean external APIs abstracting the legacy layer</h2>
    <p>
      The platform we built exposed a set of well-defined external APIs that abstracted
      the five legacy microservices entirely. Vertical teams could now build new use cases
      by composing existing API capabilities — without needing to understand or coordinate
      changes to what was underneath.
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>Designed the API contract schema in collaboration with backend and frontend
          leads — six weeks of alignment before a line of code was written</li>
      <li>Built an internal developer portal where vertical teams could browse available
          API capabilities and request additions through a structured process</li>
      <li>Migrated the highest-traffic use cases first to create early proof of concept
          before broader rollout</li>
      <li>Versioning and deprecation governance defined from the start — who could
          add to the contract, how breaking changes were handled</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">Impact</span>
    <h2>The numbers that followed</h2>
    <div class="cs-impact-box">
      <p><strong>-80% development steps</strong> for the most complex use case — from approximately 20 sequential steps to 4</p>
      <p><strong>Vertical teams unblocked</strong> — squads that had been waiting on cross-service coordination could now ship independently on supported use cases</p>
      <p><strong>Adoption enforced via policy</strong> — the legacy path was effectively closed for supported cases, preventing the two-platform maintenance problem</p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The developer portal became something unexpected: teams started requesting API
      capabilities proactively rather than building workarounds. It shifted how engineers
      thought about what the platform owed them — from a source of friction to a service
      they could make demands on.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">My role</span>
    <h2>What I specifically did</h2>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>Invented the "workflow steps" measurement system — the tool that kept
          management bought in for 12+ months without visible user-facing output</li>
      <li>Led the cross-team discovery process with all vertical PMs, producing the
          architectural requirements the platform needed to support</li>
      <li>Designed and enforced the adoption policy — the decision to make legacy
          updates require director escalation, not a request I made lightly</li>
      <li>Owned the six-week API contract alignment process, mediating between
          backend and frontend leads with different views on ownership</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">Reflection</span>
    <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
    <p>
      I underestimated how much the governance model would matter after launch.
      The technical build went well. The ongoing question of who had authority to add,
      change, or deprecate API capabilities was messier than I expected — we were
      resolving governance conflicts as they surfaced rather than having agreed rules
      upfront. I'd build a more explicit RACI for contract governance on day one,
      not once the first conflict emerged.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'd also have found a way to make the platform's contribution more legible to
      leadership. Platform work is invisible when it works — teams just move faster,
      and nobody attributes it. A simple "time-to-deploy" tracking dashboard, showing
      how long new use cases took before and after the platform, would have given us
      an ongoing narrative rather than a one-time before/after comparison.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-section" data-animate>
    <span class="cs-section-label">Also at OLX</span>
    <h2>Other significant work in this role</h2>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>
        <strong>ZAP+ integration crisis (S006)</strong> — Following OLX's acquisition
        of ZAP, the flagship cross-marketplace publishing product had a critical
        integration failure: ads published in ZAP weren't appearing in OLX. I was brought
        in to diagnose. First move: built a SQL query joining both data warehouses to
        measure the problem quantitatively (15% of accounts had at least one ad with
        an integrity issue). Root cause: OLX and ZAP had different ad lifecycle models —
        ZAP's "unpublish without delete" feature was triggering OLX's delete API.
        Accounts with integrity issues dropped from 15% to 1% after targeted fixes.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

</CaseStudyLayout>
